From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: igloo <nix@local>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 21:59:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] hw/block/pflash_cfi01: do not leak a state handler when
 post_load repeats

postload_update_cb() deletes the handler it was registered with, so a
conventional loadvm registers exactly one: load, resume, callback fires,
handler goes away.

Nothing in the vmstate contract promises a run-state change follows a
post_load, though. A caller that restores device state repeatedly without one
appends a handler per load and drops none. Every state change walks that list,
so the cost grows quadratically in the number of loads and the allocation is
unbounded. Measured with such a caller, this device's restore went from 6.5 us
to 77 us over 4,320 loads -- a 12x drift in what should be a constant.

Drop any handler still outstanding before registering the next one. Under the
conventional path pfl->vmstate is already NULL here, so this changes nothing.

License: LGPL-2.1-or-later
Patch-Set: fastsnap
---
 hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c b/hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c
index 5b9ddb20b18c1b6e950324dd0fbc1bb87d95f2e2..02187bd258e19e6f5e414fd5814f41a0b1821196 100644
--- a/hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c
+++ b/hw/block/pflash_cfi01.c
@@ -1031,6 +1031,18 @@ static int pflash_post_load(void *opaque, int version_id)
     PFlashCFI01 *pfl = opaque;
 
     if (!pfl->ro) {
+        /*
+         * postload_update_cb() removes this handler when it runs, so the usual
+         * load path registers exactly one. But nothing guarantees a state
+         * change follows a post_load -- a caller that restores device state
+         * repeatedly without one (fuzzing reset loops do exactly this) would
+         * otherwise append a handler per load and never drop any. The list is
+         * walked on every state change, so the cost is quadratic in loads and
+         * the leak is unbounded.
+         */
+        if (pfl->vmstate) {
+            qemu_del_vm_change_state_handler(pfl->vmstate);
+        }
         pfl->vmstate = qemu_add_vm_change_state_handler(postload_update_cb,
                                                         pfl);
     }
